; 44 EXTERN dispatch to an unimplemented vector is a safe no-op and
; the entry continues normally. fn 3 is XMESSAGE's reserved
; 3-parameter encoding (EXTERN offset_lsb 3 offset_msb) - the engine's
; stream walker (engine.asm) now unconditionally eats a third byte
; whenever curCondact is EXTERN and the compiled arg2 is 3, regardless
; of whether that 3 came from a real XMESSAGE (which DRC always pairs
; with a genuine third byte) or a bare hand-written EXTERN x 3 (which
; DRC compiles as a plain 2-argument EXTERN, no third byte to eat) -
; bare fn 3 is author error, not a supported classic-EXTERN form (see
; ext_xmes's header comment / MALUVA doc: "EXTERN 0 3 (with setup)").
; This check used fn 3 before ext_xmes/the stream walker existed, then
; fn 4 once ext_xmes/the stream walker existed but XPART did not. SP11
; Task 3 made vector 4 live (h_xpart) - EXTERN 5 4 would now attempt a
; real part switch to part 5 instead of hitting a stub, so this check
; moved to fn 5, still ext_stub (extVec, overlay0.asm), to keep testing
; its original, still-relevant intent: a bare EXTERN to an unimplemented
; vector compiles and no-ops. The two-part switch itself is exercised by
; NDPARTA.DSF/NDPARTB.DSF via -Part, not this suite.
> _ _   EXTERN 5 5
        MES "44 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "44 F#n"
; ---- final-review fix wave (checks 45-50) ----
; 45 REDO: sub-process PRO 16 self-restarts once, guarded by flag 113.
; Pins that REDO stores a coherent entry pointer (finding 1): the entry
; runs exactly twice, so counter 113 ends at 2.
> _ _   LET 113 0
        PROCESS 16
        EQ 113 2
        MES "45 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "45 F#n"
; 46 HASAT special values: pins the descending flag map (finding 3).
; WEARABLE 23 -> flag 57 bit 7, CONTAINER 31 -> flag 56 bit 7.
> _ _   SETCO 3                 ; hat: wearable, not container
        HASAT 23
        HASNAT 31
        SETCO 1                 ; box: container, not wearable
        HASAT 31
        HASNAT 23
        MES "46 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "46 F#n"
; 47 HASAT/HASNAT extended-attribute byte order (SP16 A5,
; docs\daad-compliance-report.md section 2 + Appendix A probe 4).
; HASAT n reads flags[59 - (n>>3)] bit (n&7), so attributes 0-7 must
; land in flag 59 and 8-15 in flag 58; the DDB stores the pair as one
; LITTLE-ENDIAN word, so flag 59 takes the FIRST file byte. The lamp
; carries attribute 0 alone (bytes 01 00) and the coin attribute 8
; alone (bytes 00 01) - see the /OBJ column note above - so this
; check disambiguates the two directions: an interpreter with the
; bytes crossed passes NEITHER half.
; HISTORY: this check used to assert HASAT 0 on the coin, under a
; comment claiming the 16 /OBJ columns were per-byte MSB-first (attr
; 0 = 8th column). That reading of the column order was wrong - the
; coin's 8th-column Y is attribute 8 - and the check passed only
; because the interpreter's byte order was crossed the same way. Both
; are fixed together; do not "restore" either half alone.
> _ _   SETCO 0                 ; lamp: attribute 0 only
        HASAT 0
        HASNAT 1
        HASNAT 8
        SETCO 2                 ; coin: attribute 8 only
        HASAT 8
        HASNAT 9
        HASNAT 0
        MES "47 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "47 F#n"
; 48 PLACE 255 -> HERE (finding 7): coin placed at 255 must land at the
; player's location (flag 38), not raise error 2.
> _ _   RESET
        GOTO 0
        PLACE 2 255
        COPYOF 2 114
        SAME 114 38
        ISAT 2 0
        MES "48 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "48 F#n"
; 49 GET capacity refusal cancels DOALL (finding 5): with MAXCARR 0,
; every GET refuses SM27. DOALL over loc 0 (box + hat) must stop after
; the first refusal, so counter 115 stays at 1 (without the fix it
; iterates every object).
> _ _   RESET
        LET 37 0                ; MAXCARR = 0 -> hands always full
        LET 44 255              ; Noun2/Adj2 clear of any object
        LET 45 255
        LET 115 0
        GOTO 0
        PROCESS 17
        EQ 115 1
        MES "49 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "49 F#n"
; 50 QUIT (owner-interactive): SM12 prompt. Press Y -> "50 OK";
; press N (or any non-Y) -> "50 F" then continues. Kept last.
> _ _   QUIT
        MES "50 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "50 F#n"
; ---- interactive parser checks (owner types at each prompt) ----
; DAAD PARSE semantics: a VALID sentence makes PARSE fail as a
; condition (aborting the entry - the remainder of a PARSE entry is
; the game's invalid-input/timeout path) and marks done; unparseable
; input or a timeout makes PARSE pass. Assertions therefore live in
; the entry AFTER each PARSE.
; 51 basic verb+noun
> _ _   MES "51 TYPE: get lamp#n"
        PARSE 0
        MES "51 F#n"
        SKIP 2
> _ _   EQ 33 100
        EQ 34 51
        EQ 35 255
        MES "51 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "51 F#n"
; 52 adjective + compound second order parses independently
> _ _   MES "52 TYPE: get small lamp and drop hat#n"
        PARSE 0
        MES "52A F#n"
        SKIP 2
> _ _   EQ 33 100
        EQ 34 51
        EQ 35 202
        MES "52A OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "52A F#n"
> _ _   PARSE 0
        MES "52B F#n"
        SKIP 2
> _ _   EQ 33 101
        EQ 34 53
        MES "52B OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "52B F#n"
; 53 compound implicit verb (previousVerb)
> _ _   MES "53 TYPE: get lamp and hat#n"
        PARSE 0
        MES "53 F#n"
        SKIP 3
> _ _   PARSE 0
        MES "53 F#n"
        SKIP 2
> _ _   EQ 33 100
        EQ 34 53
        MES "53 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "53 F#n"
; 54 pronoun: LAMP (id 51 >= 50) remembered, IT substitutes
> _ _   MES "54 TYPE: examine lamp#n"
        PARSE 0
        MES "54 F#n"
        SKIP 3
> _ _   MES "54 TYPE: get it#n"
        PARSE 0
        MES "54 F#n"
        SKIP 2
> _ _   EQ 33 100
        EQ 34 51
        MES "54 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "54 F#n"
; 55 noun-as-verb (NORTH noun id 2 < 20)
> _ _   MES "55 TYPE: north#n"
        PARSE 0
        MES "55 F#n"
        SKIP 2
> _ _   EQ 33 2
        MES "55 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "55 F#n"
; 56 unknown word skipped
> _ _   MES "56 TYPE: get xyzzy lamp#n"
        PARSE 0
        MES "56 F#n"
        SKIP 2
> _ _   EQ 33 100
        EQ 34 51
        MES "56 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "56 F#n"
; 57 noun2 + preposition + object-2 resolution (box = object 1,
; container, at location 0; BOX noun id 50). Flags 39/40 are the
; SECOND referenced object's extended attribute pair and are the
; third A5 site (obj2_resolve, overlay1.asm) - the only one HASAT
; cannot reach, since HASAT always reads the 58/59 pair. The box
; carries attribute 8 alone (bytes 00 01), so flag 39 (attrs 8-15)
; must read 1 and flag 40 (attrs 0-7) must read 0; crossed bytes
; give exactly the opposite pair.
> _ _   MES "57 TYPE: get lamp in box#n"
        GOTO 0
        PARSE 0
        MES "57 F#n"
        SKIP 2
> _ _   EQ 43 203
        EQ 44 50
        EQ 25 1
        EQ 26 128
        EQ 27 0
        EQ 39 1
        EQ 40 0
        MES "57 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "57 F#n"
; 58 input timeout: PARSE passes on timeout, flag 49 = 128 exactly
> _ _   MES "58 WAIT - DO NOT TYPE#n"
        TIME 2 0
        PARSE 0
        EQ 49 128
        TIME 0 0
        MES "58 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "58 F#n"
; 59 NEWTEXT discards the pending tail
> _ _   MES "59 TYPE: get lamp and drop hat#n"
        PARSE 0
        MES "59 F#n"
        SKIP 3
> _ _   NEWTEXT
        MES "59 TYPE: north#n"
        PARSE 0
        MES "59 F#n"
        SKIP 2
> _ _   EQ 33 2
        MES "59 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "59 F#n"
; 60 conjunctions neutralised beyond the first separator
> _ _   MES "60 TYPE: get lamp, drop hat and get box#n"
        PARSE 0
        MES "60 F#n"
        SKIP 4
> _ _   PARSE 0
        MES "60 F#n"
        SKIP 3
> _ _   EQ 33 101
        EQ 34 53
        PARSE 0
        MES "60 F#n"
        SKIP 2
> _ _   EQ 33 100
        EQ 34 50
        MES "60 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "60 F#n"
; ---- persistence checks ----
; A6 RAMLOAD with nothing saved: no-op
> _ _   LET 120 7
        RAMLOAD 200
        EQ 120 7
        MES "A6 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "A6 F#n"
; 61 RAMSAVE/RAMLOAD partial restore (inclusive bound)
> _ _   LET 116 11
        LET 117 22
        PLACE 2 1               ; coin into the box
        RAMSAVE
        LET 116 99
        LET 117 88
        PLACE 2 0
        RAMLOAD 116
        EQ 116 11               ; flag 116 <= bound: restored
        EQ 117 88               ; flag 117 above bound: preserved
        ISAT 2 1                ; object location restored
        MES "61 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "61 F#n"
; 62 SAVE then LOAD round-trip (owner types the same name twice)
> _ _   MES "62 TYPE NAME: t1#n"
        LET 118 77
        SAVE 0
        LET 118 5
        MES "62 TYPE NAME: t1#n"
        LOAD 0
        EQ 118 77
        MES "62 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "62 F#n"
; 63 LOAD of a missing file: SM57, entry aborts (LOAD is condition-
; typed), state untouched, session continues. sav_read is atomic
; (staged commit), so EVERY load failure behaves this way - there is
; no destructive path.
> _ _   LET 119 42
        MES "63 TYPE NAME: nofile#n"
        LOAD 0
        MES "63 F#n"
        SKIP 2
> _ _   EQ 119 42
        MES "63 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "63 F#n"
; ---- graphics checks ----
; 64 PICTURE with no image file on SD: fails as a condition, entry
; aborts, session continues. ASSUMES the suite SD state has no 200.*
; art - the Rabenstein set staged by build-tests.ps1 -Rab covers
; 000-020 only, and nothing else stages picture files (confirmed at
; build time: sd\ holds no 200.*). The loader probes the whole
; extension chain (NX2.ZX0/N2Z/NX2/NXI.ZX0/NXZ/NXI), every probe
; misses, gfx_load fails clean and h_picture reports false.
> _ _   PICTURE 200
        MES "64 F#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "64 OK#n"
; ---- audio checks ----
; Audio QUALITY is owner-ear (CSpect + real hardware legs); these
; assert safe completion only.
; 65 BEEP: a valid note returns after blocking ~0.1s; an odd tone
; and a zero duration are silent no-ops that still complete.
; SP16 A3/A4 - DRC WRITES BEEP AS "BEEP duration tone" AND THEN SWAPS
; THE TWO FOR EVERY ZX TARGET (drb.php:900-914), so "BEEP 10 60"
; below reaches h_beep as arg1 = tone 60, arg2 = duration 6 (10
; centiseconds scaled by the target's 120/200 factor). The source
; order here is therefore unchanged, but its MEANING at the handler
; is the reverse of what it used to be read as - before A3 this
; whole check was a silent no-op, because h_beep read tone 60 as a
; duration and duration 6 as a tone and rejected 6 as out of range.
; DRC also rewrites any BEEP whose source tone falls outside 48..238
; into a PAUSE before the swap, so 48 and 238 are the extremes a
; database can actually carry - 238 is the A4 ceiling raise (was 222)
; and exercises aud_periods.inc entries 100-107 for the first time.
; Audio QUALITY is the owner ear leg; this asserts completion only.
> _ _   BEEP 10 60
        BEEP 10 61
        BEEP 0 60
        BEEP 10 48
        BEEP 10 238
        MES "65 OK#n"
; 66 SFX: missing song no-ops (the suite SD carries no 200.AKY),
; play-effect with no effects bank no-ops (the suite SD carries no
; GAME.SFB either - the bank guard must swallow it, not play garbage),
; unknown sub-command no-ops, stop-music with nothing playing no-ops.
> _ _   SFX 200 6
        SFX 1 1
        SFX 1 99
        SFX 200 8
        MES "66 OK#n"
; 67 SFX 3/4 as 1/2, stream intact: subs 3 and 4 probe NNN.WAV first
; exactly like subs 1/2 (DRC's DRF 0.40 cannot emit a DOS-source rate
; byte at plan-time - the pass-through keeps 3/4 usable at runtime).
; No 200.WAV and no GAME.SFB in suite staging, so both fall through
; the sample probe AND the AY effect fallback and land as silent
; no-ops. Witness flag proves the condacts after them still ran.
> _ _   LET 123 91
        SFX 200 3
        SFX 200 4
        EQ 123 91
        MES "67 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "67 F#n"
; 68 SFX 0 5: stop-both-kinds with nothing playing - idle no-op,
; session continues.
> _ _   LET 123 92
        SFX 0 5
        EQ 123 92
        MES "68 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "68 F#n"
; 69 SFX 99 1: 099.WAV is the malformed fixture (16 bytes, no RIFF
; header) staged by build-tests.ps1 -Suite. aud_load_wav's RIFF probe
; rejects it (CF set), h_sfx falls through to the AY effect path -
; also a no-op, the suite SD carries no GAME.SFB. Silent end to end.
> _ _   LET 123 93
        SFX 99 1
        EQ 123 93
        MES "69 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "69 F#n"
; 70 SFX 98 1: 098.WAV is the truncated-WAV fixture (valid 44-byte
; RIFF+fmt+data header, no payload bytes) staged by build-tests.ps1
; -Suite. aud_load_wav's RIFF/fmt probes pass, but the streaming read
; comes up short against the promised data size and rejects (CF set) -
; h_sfx falls through to the AY effect path, also a no-op (no
; GAME.SFB). Silent end to end.
> _ _   LET 123 94
        SFX 98 1
        EQ 123 94
        MES "70 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "70 F#n"
; 71 XUNDONE (EXTERN 0 7): PRO 18's entry prints a witness message then
; runs EXTERN 0 7 and falls through its terminator (no DONE/NOTDONE) -
; the MES action stamps isDone=1 pre-dispatch, XUNDONE's ext_undone then
; clears it back to 0. The table then runs out (single-entry process)
; and the pop leaves isDone exactly as PRO 18 left it, which is what
; the containment buys: ISNDONE here must pass. Also pins that the
; new EXTERN fn-3 stream-consumption (curCondact 61, arg2 3) leaves the
; plain 2-arg EXTERN 0 7 encoding alone (arg2 7 <> 3, no 3rd byte read).
> _ _   PROCESS 18
        ISNDONE
        MES "71 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "71 F#n"
; 72 XMESSAGE (EXTERN vector 3): the real meta-condact, so DRC emits
; both the 3-parameter EXTERN (offset_lsb, 3, offset_msb) and the
; 0.XMB entry (staged as sd\SUITE\0.XMB by build-tests.ps1 -Suite).
; ext_xmes claims the XMES pool bank, seeks/reads 0.XMB and prints
; through the standard DDB text machinery (rd_seek_page). EXTERN is
; action-typed, so the engine never gates entry continuation on its
; result - the witness EQ right after XMESSAGE is what actually
; proves the 3-byte stream discipline held (flag 123 survives the
; dispatch intact); the printed text itself is the owner eye-leg's
; evidence, not asserted here.
> _ _   LET 123 95
        XMESSAGE "72 external text"
        EQ 123 95
        MES "72 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "72 F#n"
; 73 GFX: the brief-pinned safe-sub witness - clear front (5), swap
; front/back (2), unknown sub (99) - buffer/register ops only, no
; picture ever staged/displayed in this suite (check 64's PICTURE 200
; fails clean), so l2Mode/l2FrontBank/l2BackBank sit at cold-boot
; defaults throughout. Safe subs only, no assertion on pixels - visual
; proof is the owner eye-leg (PICTURE, then GFX 1/5/0) in the task
; report, not here. Witness flag proves the condacts after them still
; ran.
> _ _   LET 123 96
        GFX 0 5
        GFX 0 2
        GFX 0 99
        EQ 123 96
        MES "73 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "73 F#n"
; 74 GFX: smoke coverage for the new copy loop and the remaining
; implemented subs - front<->back copy (1 then 0, net no lasting
; surface-role change), clear back (6), numbered-palette no-op (9).
; Same no-pixel-assertion rule as check 73.
> _ _   LET 123 97
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 6
        GFX 0 9
        EQ 123 97
        MES "74 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "74 F#n"
; ---- streamed-song checks (SP10 client 2: AYS) ----
; 75 SFX n 6 with no NNN.AYS and no NNN.AKY: the stream probe misses,
; the AKY song probe misses, SFX no-ops and the entry continues. The
; suite SD carries no 200.AYS/200.AKY, so both loaders fail clean.
> _ _   LET 123 98
        SFX 200 6
        EQ 123 98
        MES "75 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "75 F#n"
; 76 SFX 2 7 loads + loops the staged 002.AYS stream (build-tests.ps1
; -Suite stages it from tools\audio_assets). The PAUSE lets the ISR
; consume the start request and tick the stream for real frames BEFORE
; SFX 0 8 stops it - without the gap both mailbox bits would land in the
; same frame and the fixed stop-before-start order would restart, not
; stop. Headless-safe: assert only that the entry continues across the
; sequence (witness flag survives) - playback is the owner ear leg.
; Robust either way: if the stream fails to claim, SFX falls through to
; the (absent) 002.AKY and still no-ops.
> _ _   LET 123 99
        SFX 2 7
        PAUSE 10
        SFX 0 8
        EQ 123 99
        MES "76 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "76 F#n"
; ---- SP10 Task 5: MOUSE + CALL closure ----
; 77 MOUSE 3 into scratch flags 130-133 (buttons, X/8, Y/8, X/6): the
; suite's first (and only) MOUSE poll finds mouseBaseSet clear, so it
; only latches the raw Kempston baseline and leaves the accumulated
; position at its init value (mouseX=160, mouseY=128) - deterministic
; no matter what headless CSpect's emulated ports return. That makes
; this a positive control, not a witness-only smoke check: flags
; 131-133 are asserted against mouseX/8=20, mouseY/8=16, mouseX/6=26,
; so a stub that leaves the flags untouched fails this check. Flag
; 130 (buttons) stays unasserted - the raw Kempston button port's
; idle value under headless CSpect is not deterministic. Flags
; 130-133 sit clear of every other flag this suite reads/writes
; (witness 123, RANDOM's 107, PRINT's 108, the REDO counter's 113).
; Visual pointer verification (MOUSE 1 show/track, MOUSE 2 hide) is
; the owner eye leg, not here.
> _ _   LET 123 100
        MOUSE 130 3
        EQ 131 20
        EQ 132 16
        EQ 133 26
        EQ 123 100
        MES "77 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "77 F#n"
; 78 CALL: documented no-op (jdaad parity - jdaad.js's own _CALL() is
; the same shape). Proves the entry continues through it exactly like
; any other action condact.
> _ _   LET 123 101
        CALL 0 0
        EQ 123 101
        MES "78 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "78 F#n"
; 79 XMESSAGE nonzero offset: SECOND XMESSAGE in this DSF - DRC
; appends each XMESSAGE's bytes to 0.XMB in source order, so this
; entry lands after check 72's "72 external text" and its terminator,
; guaranteeing a nonzero byte offset (unlike check 72's own entry,
; which happens to sit at offset 0 as the first XMESSAGE compiled).
; Exercises ext_xmes's esx_fseek-to-nonzero-offset path rather than
; just the trivial seek-to-0 path check 72 already covers. Witness
; idiom as 72: EXTERN is action-typed, so the flag EQ right after
; XMESSAGE is what actually proves the 3-byte stream discipline held
; at a nonzero offset; the printed text itself is the owner eye-leg's
; evidence, not asserted here.
> _ _   LET 123 102
        XMESSAGE "79 external text two"
        EQ 123 102
        MES "79 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "79 F#n"
; 80 SAVE/LOAD with a dirty name: punctuation and lowercase must
; survive sav_fname_sanitize's mapping (overlay1.asm) into a
; filesystem-safe 8.3 name - lowercase -> uppercase, alphanumerics
; pass, everything else -> 'X' (the DAAD manual only promises the
; file name is "MADE acceptable" on 8-bit targets - DAAD_Manual_1991
; .md - no algorithm given, so this is the pinned choice). Owner
; types the SAME dirty name both times when prompted:
;     my.sv!
; sav_fname drops nothing here (no spaces) and uppercases the two
; letters; sav_fname_sanitize then replaces '.' and '!' with 'X', so
; both SAVE and LOAD resolve to the same MYXSVX.SAV - a plain A-Z0-9
; 8.3 name - regardless of the raw punctuation typed. The round trip
; below (118 corrupted between SAVE and LOAD, then asserted restored)
; proves the SAME file was written and read back, i.e. LOAD sanitized
; the retyped dirty name identically to SAVE. A pass is: no "File
; name error." (SM59) or "I/O Error" (SM57) printed on either action,
; and EQ 118 77 holds after LOAD.
> _ _   MES "80 TYPE NAME: my.sv!#n"
        LET 118 77
        SAVE 0
        LET 118 5
        MES "80 TYPE NAME: my.sv!#n"
        LOAD 0
        EQ 118 77
        MES "80 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "80 F#n"
; ---- SP16 Task 1 appended checks (81-) ----
; Appended deliberately: the differential parser harness
; (tests\parser) indexes its findings by turn, and every turn it
; replays lands in checks 50-60, so anything added after check 80 is
; unreachable from the replay and leaves the baseline turn numbering
; untouched.
; 81 INKEY clears flag 61. DAAD's INKEY publishes the key code in
; flag 60 (fKey1) and the IBM extended code in flag 61 (fKey2);
; both references clear 61 whenever they write 60, and h_inkey used
; to write 60 alone, leaving whatever was in 61 (compliance report
; section 4, "Flag 61"). Both flags are pre-poisoned so a handler
; that skipped either write fails this check.
; The PAUSE lets a key still physically down from check 80's typed
; file name release before INKEY samples the matrix - INKEY is a
; condition and its result is deliberately NOT gated on here: the
; assertions live in the NEXT entry, so they run whether INKEY found
; a key (entry falls off its end) or not (entry aborts at INKEY).
> _ _   PAUSE 50
        LET 60 66
        LET 61 55
        INKEY
> _ _   ZERO 60                 ; INKEY overwrote the poison (no key
                                ; down at the sample -> 0)
        ZERO 61                 ; and cleared fKey2 alongside it
        MES "81 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "81 F#n"
; ---- SP16 Task 2 appended checks (82-86) ----
; Same placement rule as 81 above: after check 80, so the differential
; harness's turn indexing is untouched. These pin the FLAG 1 arithmetic
; of the object-interaction wave (GET/DROP/WEAR/REMOVE/PUTIN/TAKEOUT
; all print system messages now, and all of them move the object AND
; adjust flag 1 on the success path only). The messages themselves are
; a text-channel matter and are evidenced by the harness, not here.
; 82 GET/DROP round trip: flag 1 up on the way in, down on the way out.
> _ _   RESET                   ; lamp carried, box + hat at loc 0
        GOTO 0
        ABILITY 10 100          ; room and strength for anything here
        EQ 1 1
        GET 3
        CARRIED 3
        EQ 1 2
        DROP 3
        ISAT 3 0
        EQ 1 1
        MES "82 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "82 F#n"
; 83 WEAR decrements flag 1 (worn is not carried), REMOVE increments it
; back. The hat must be CARRIED first - WEAR on an object lying at the
; player's location now refuses SM49 instead of wearing it.
> _ _   GET 3
        EQ 1 2
        WEAR 3
        WORN 3
        EQ 1 1
        REMOVE 3
        CARRIED 3
        EQ 1 2
        MES "83 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "83 F#n"
; 84 PUTIN decrements flag 1, TAKEOUT increments it. Object 1 (the box)
; is the container; TAKEOUT now runs a weight test too, so the strength
; set in 82 has to still cover the hat.
> _ _   PUTIN 3 1
        ISAT 3 1
        EQ 1 1
        TAKEOUT 3 1
        CARRIED 3
        EQ 1 2
        MES "84 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "84 F#n"
; 85 a REFUSED GET changes nothing: flag 1 stands, the object stays put,
; and the refusal's DONE aborts the rest of the entry. The refused GET
; is CONTAINED in PRO 20 - the refusal's DONE pops a whole PROCESS, so
; running it inline here would abort PRO 1 itself and take checks 86
; and the SUITES DONE tail with it. Same containment pattern as checks
; 49 and 86 (PRO 17 / PRO 19).
> _ _   DROP 3
        LET 37 0                ; hands always full -> SM27
        LET 124 0
        PROCESS 20
> _ _   ABILITY 10 100
        ZERO 124
        EQ 1 1
        ISAT 3 0
        MES "85 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "85 F#n"
; 86 weight is tested BEFORE the hands-full count (compliance report
; section 3, GET). This is the one ordering difference the state
; channel can see: SM43 (too heavy) does NOT cancel a DOALL, SM27
; (hands full) does. Both conditions are made true at once - strength
; 0 and capacity 0 - so a weight-first GET refuses SM43 per object and
; the DOALL runs to exhaustion (both objects at loc 0 counted), while a
; count-first GET refuses SM27 and cancels after the first.
> _ _   RESET
        GOTO 0
        LET 37 0                ; capacity 0 - hands full
        LET 52 0                ; strength 0 - everything too heavy
        LET 44 255              ; Noun2/Adj2 clear of any object
        LET 45 255
        LET 125 0
        PROCESS 19
        EQ 125 2
        MES "86 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "86 F#n"
; ---- SP16 Task 3 appended checks (87-96) ----
; Object resolution: D1 (noun-only matching), B17 (WHATO null
; semantics), B11/B12 (SWAP/COPYOO referenced object and flag 1), B29
; (zero-weight containers), B10 (AUTOT's search chain). Same placement
; rule as 81-86 - appended after check 80, so the differential
; harness's turn indexing is untouched. Objects 4-9 (see /OBJ) exist
; only for these checks and are CREATEd here, never earlier.
;
; 87 D1: a bare noun against objects that all have adjectives.
; Noun1 = SWORD, no adjective. Objects 4 (RUSTY) and 5 (SHINY) share
; the noun and NEITHER is adjectiveless, so before D1 the matcher
; rejected both and flag 51 came back 255 - "GET SWORD" could not
; reach a rusty sword. jDAAD records object 4 as a PARTIAL match and,
; finding no full match anywhere in the pass, returns it.
> _ _   RESET
        GOTO 0
        ABILITY 10 100          ; check 86 left capacity and strength 0
        CREATE 4
        CREATE 5
        SETCO 0                 ; poison flag 51 with a REAL object, so
        LET 34 54               ; a handler that writes nothing fails
        LET 35 255
        WHATO
        EQ 51 4
        MES "87 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "87 F#n"
; 88 the adjective still disambiguates. SHINY SWORD must take object 5
; even though object 4 shares the noun and comes first in the table -
; this is what a blanket "ignore the adjective when it matches nothing"
; rule would break, and why jDAAD's partial model was copied instead.
> _ _   LET 35 205              ; Adj1 = SHINY
        WHATO
        EQ 51 5
        MES "88 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "88 F#n"
; 89 full beats partial WITHIN one pass. Bare ROBE meets object 6
; (SMALL ROBE) first and records it as a partial, then must override it
; with object 7, which has no adjective and is therefore a full match.
; Table order is deliberately partial-first, so a first-match-wins
; resolver answers 6 and fails here.
> _ _   CREATE 6
        CREATE 7
        LET 34 55               ; Noun1 = ROBE
        LET 35 255
        WHATO
        EQ 51 7
        MES "89 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "89 F#n"
; 90 and because partial/full resolves inside ONE location pass, the
; search chain's location priority still wins: with the SMALL ROBE
; CARRIED and the adjectiveless ROBE merely lying here, the carried
; pass returns its partial (6) and the here pass, which holds the full
; match, is never reached. A resolver that scanned all locations first
; and picked the best match globally would answer 7.
> _ _   GET 6
        CARRIED 6
        LET 34 55
        LET 35 255
        WHATO
        EQ 51 6
        MES "90 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "90 F#n"
; 91 B17: a WHATO that finds nothing is referencedObject(NULLWORD) -
; flag 51 = 255 AND flags 54-59 ZEROED (msx2daad substitutes an
; all-zero nullObject). All six are pre-poisoned, so the old handler,
; which wrote 51 alone and left 54-59 holding the previous object's
; data, fails this.
> _ _   LET 54 11
        LET 55 22
        LET 56 33
        LET 57 44
        LET 58 55
        LET 59 66
        LET 34 2                ; Noun1 = NORTH - no object has it
        LET 35 255
        WHATO
        EQ 51 255
        ZERO 54
        ZERO 55
        ZERO 56
        ZERO 57
        ZERO 58
        ZERO 59
        MES "91 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "91 F#n"
; 92 B11: SWAP of two CARRIED objects must leave flag 1 alone - the
; manual says "Flag 1 is not adjusted" outright. The old two-obj_move
; implementation decremented once per object with no matching
; increment, so this landed on 0 instead of 2. SWAP must also set the
; referenced object to objno 2, which it never did at all.
> _ _   SETCO 0                 ; flag 51 = 0 so EQ 51 6 below is real
        CARRIED 0               ; the lamp
        CARRIED 6               ; the small robe, from check 90
        EQ 1 2
        SWAP 0 6
        EQ 1 2
        CARRIED 0
        CARRIED 6
        EQ 51 6
        MES "92 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "92 F#n"
; 93 the exchange itself still works after losing obj_move: a carried
; object and one lying here trade places. Flag 1 stays 2 either way
; here (one carried object leaves as another arrives) - it is the
; locations and the referenced object this pins.
> _ _   ISAT 7 0                ; the adjectiveless robe is lying here
        SWAP 6 7
        ISAT 6 0
        CARRIED 7
        EQ 1 2
        EQ 51 7
        MES "93 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "93 F#n"
; 94 B12: COPYOO keeps its move - jDAAD routes COPYOO through PLACE,
; which does adjust flag 1 - but must also set the referenced object to
; objno 2, which the manual requires of COPYOO exactly as it does of
; SWAP.
> _ _   SETCO 0
        COPYOO 6 7              ; robe 7 joins robe 6 at location 0
        ISAT 7 0
        EQ 1 1                  ; robe 7 stopped being carried
        EQ 51 7
        MES "94 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "94 F#n"
; 95 B29, the manual's "magic bag": a container of ZERO own weight
; transmits zero for its contents too, so the weight recursion must not
; descend into it. Object 8 weighs 0 and is a container, object 9
; weighs 5 and goes inside it. The positive control is check 27, where
; the box (own weight 2) DOES add its contents.
> _ _   CREATE 8
        CREATE 9
        LET 111 8
        COPYFO 111 9            ; the gem's location = object 8, i.e.
                                ; inside the bag. PUTIN 9 8 would be the
                                ; natural way to say this, but DRF
                                ; validates a PUTIN container parameter
                                ; as a LOCATION number and there is no
                                ; location 8 - COPYFO writes the same
                                ; byte with no such check.
        LET 112 99
        WEIGH 8 112
        ZERO 112
        WEIGH 9 112
        EQ 112 5                ; the gem on its own still weighs 5
        MES "95 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "95 F#n"
; 96 B10: AUTOT's fallback chain is container, carried, worn, here -
; and then it STOPS. It used to end in obj_find_n1's anywhere pass,
; which reached objects the player was nowhere near; TAKEOUT refused
; them, but not before making them the referenced object. Object 4 is
; parked inside object 3 while the player stays at location 0 and the
; searched container is object 1, so an anywhere pass is the only thing
; that can see it. Note object 5 (SHINY SWORD) IS here and shares the
; noun - it is rejected on the adjective, which keeps the here pass
; honest. Contained in PRO 21 because the AUTOT refusal performs DONE -
; same reason as check 85's PRO 20.
> _ _   SETCO 0
        LET 111 3
        COPYFO 111 4            ; object 4 -> inside object 3
        LET 34 54               ; Noun1 = SWORD
        LET 35 204              ; Adj1 = RUSTY - object 4 exactly
        LET 126 0
        PROCESS 21
> _ _   EQ 51 0                 ; nothing was referenced
        COPYOF 4 110
        EQ 110 3                ; and nothing moved
        ZERO 126
        MES "96 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "96 F#n"
; ---- SP16 Task 4 appended checks (97-102) ----
; Same placement rule as 81 and 82-86 and 87-96: appended after check
; 80, so the differential harness's turn indexing is untouched.
; These pin the FLAG side of the listing and flow-control wave. The
; listing FORM itself (continuous versus one-per-line) is a text-channel
; property and is evidenced by the harness transcript, not here.
;
; Location 1 is the scratch location for 97-100: the suite's only other
; location, and nothing is parked there (check 33's PLACE 2 1 puts the
; coin back with PLACE 2 0 four lines later). Check 97 does not assume
; that - it PROVES it, and fails loudly if some future check leaves an
; object behind.
;
; 97 B16: flag 53 bit 7 ("objects were listed") must be MAINTAINED on
; the LISTAT path, both ways. It used to be touched only by LISTOBJ:
; LISTAT never set it when it listed something and never cleared it
; when it listed nothing, which is the flag 53 divergence in
; parser-bugs entry 5. Both references maintain it for both condacts
; (jdaad.js:2379 listObjects sets 0x80 / clears it in the else arm;
; daad_condacts.c:1922 _internal_listat clears F53_LISTED up front and
; ORs it in per object printed).
; The empty LISTAT here also exercises "SM53 alone" - NextDAAD used to
; add a newline after it that neither reference produces. That is a
; text-channel effect; only the bit is assertable from DSF.
> _ _   LET 53 0                ; clear fOFlags, then let LISTAT drive it
        LISTAT 1                ; nothing at location 1 -> SM53 alone
        LT 53 128               ; bit 7 CLEAR: the empty LISTAT cleared it
        PLACE 8 1               ; the bag becomes the only object there
        LISTAT 1
        GT 53 127               ; bit 7 SET from the LISTAT path too
        MES "97 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "97 F#n"
; 98 B16: bit 6 selects the listing form and list_at must READ it
; without disturbing it. Bit 6 set = the continuous "a, b and c." form;
; bit 6 clear = one object per line, which is the DEFAULT (flag 53
; starts at 0) and is the form NextDAAD never produced because it
; forced the continuous one. Here the bag is listed both ways; what is
; assertable is that bit 6 survives and bit 7 lands on top of it.
> _ _   LET 53 64               ; bit 6: continuous listing
        LISTAT 1
        EQ 53 192               ; bit 6 intact, bit 7 added
        LET 53 0                ; back to the one-per-line default
        LISTAT 1
        EQ 53 128               ; bit 6 still clear, bit 7 still set
        MES "98 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "98 F#n"
; 99 B14 "caso A": a DOALL whose INITIAL search finds nothing performs
; NEWTEXT and NOTDONE. It used to plain-pop, and because DOALL is
; action-typed the dispatcher had already set isDone to 1, so an empty
; DOALL exited the table as DONE. Contained in PRO 22 so the NOTDONE
; lands on a table of its own - the PROCESS 22 push clears isDone, so
; the three actions above it cannot mask the result the way they would
; in a bare entry - the same containment pattern as checks 85 and 96.
; The NEWTEXT half (the rest of a typed compound order is discarded) is
; not assertable here, since it needs a compound order on the input
; buffer: that is the harness's compound-abort script and the owner's
; interactive leg.
> _ _   PLACE 8 0               ; location 1 empty again
        LET 53 0
        LISTAT 1
        LT 53 128               ; precondition PROVEN: nothing at loc 1
        PROCESS 22              ; PRO 22 is a bare DOALL 1
        ISNDONE                 ; caso A must exit the table NOTDONE
        MES "99 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "99 F#n"
; 100 B14 "caso B", the regression guard on 99: a DOALL that DID
; iterate and has now run out must still complete the level as DONE.
; Both references keep the two cases apart - msx2daad's
; _internal_doall_continue carries the comment "No isDone/checkEntry/
; lsBuffer mutation here (caso B per PCDAAD spec)" and its unit suite
; carries D-CANCEL-2d, "DOALL exhausted *after iterating* must mark
; DONE" - and NextDAAD reaches both through the one
; eng_doall_next.exhausted label, so the split is by the entry value of
; doallObj. Merging them would make a SUCCESSFUL "DROP ALL" answer
; "I can't do that" and kill the rest of the player's order.
> _ _   PLACE 8 1               ; exactly one object at location 1
        PROCESS 23              ; PRO 23 is a bare DOALL 1
        ISDONE                  ; caso B: iterated, then exhausted = DONE
        PLACE 8 0               ; leave location 1 empty behind us
        MES "100 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "100 F#n"
; 101 B28: an out-of-range window number is IGNORED and the current
; window stays selected. h_window masked the number with 7, so WINDOW 8
; silently selected window 0 - a wrong window rather than a no-op. Both
; references bail out (daad_condacts.c:1674 "if (window >= WINDOWS_NUM)
; return;", jdaad.js:3455 "if (Parameter1 < NUM_WINDOWS)").
; Flag 63 (fCurWin) is the observable. Nothing is printed between the
; WINDOW calls, so the suite's own output stays in window 0.
; The out-of-range value is passed INDIRECTLY: DRF rejects a literal
; "WINDOW 8" at compile time ("Invalid window number, must be in the
; 0-7 range"), so a literal cannot reach the interpreter at all. The
; runtime guard is what this pins, and msx2daad's do_WINDOW reads its
; parameter with getValueOrIndirection() before the same range test,
; so the indirect form is the reference-faithful way to express it.
> _ _   LET 110 8
        WINDOW 1
        EQ 63 1
        WINDOW @110             ; out of range: ignored (was 8 AND 7 = 0)
        EQ 63 1                 ; window 1 is STILL selected
        WINDOW 0                ; restore the suite's window before MES
        EQ 63 0
        MES "101 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "101 F#n"
; 102 B18 (OWNER LEG - typed verb, never fires automatically): EXIT n
; with n non-zero is a full reset and restart, not a no-op. It was
; routed to h_unimpl. There is no way to assert this in-suite: a
; restart clears every flag and re-enters PRO 0, so any witness flag is
; gone by the time anything could read it, and the suite would relaunch
; itself forever. So the check is inverted and gated on a TYPED verb -
; type XEXIT at the prompt:
;   PASS = the screen clears and the suite runs again from check 1
;          (flags cleared, objects reinitialised, PRO 0 re-entered).
;   FAIL = "102 F" prints and the turn continues normally, which is
;          exactly what the old h_unimpl no-op did.
; The second entry is reachable ONLY if EXIT 1 returned to its caller.
> XEXIT _ MES "102: EXIT 1 - expect a FULL RESTART now#n"
        EXIT 1
> XEXIT _ MES "102 F - EXIT 1 was a no-op#n"
; ---- Wave C appended checks (103-104) ----
; Same placement rule as 81-102: appended after them, so nothing before
; here shifts. These two are about the PAGER, which no earlier check
; exercises deliberately - every page this suite produces up to now is
; incidental, a side effect of a screenful of OK lines.
;
; 103 the pager itself: printing more lines than the window holds must
; fire "More...", wait, erase the prompt and CONTINUE THE ENTRY. The
; witness flag is what makes that last part assertable - prn_more_check
; (src/print.asm) saves and restores the outer reader position, its
; in-flight push depth, the MMU-save shadow and the physical MMU slot
; around the nested SM32 print, and a fault in any of that corrupts the
; message stream the entry is still reading from rather than announcing
; itself. 40 lines against a 32-row window (25 on jDAAD) guarantees at
; least one page on both. The lines are NUMBERED rather than repeated
; filler: the harness recovers a turn's new text by working out how far
; the tilemap scrolled (tests/parser/tilemap.py scroll_delta), and forty
; IDENTICAL rows make that shift ambiguous - measured, it silently
; dropped nine of them. Distinct rows make the page countable, which is
; the whole point of the check.
; This is also the harness's live test of breakpoint-driven page capture
; (tests/parser/nleg.py): a page fired here is captured by the emulator
; at the pager's park point, so the lines below must appear in the
; replay transcript IN FULL.
> _ _   LET 123 103
        MES "103 PAGER#n"
        MES "01#n02#n03#n04#n05#n06#n07#n08#n09#n10#n"
        MES "11#n12#n13#n14#n15#n16#n17#n18#n19#n20#n"
        MES "21#n22#n23#n24#n25#n26#n27#n28#n29#n30#n"
        MES "31#n32#n33#n34#n35#n36#n37#n38#n39#n40#n"
        EQ 123 103
        MES "103 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "103 F#n"
; 104 the pager's TIMEOUT. TIME's second parameter is flag 49, the
; timeout control byte: bit 1 arms the "More..." wait specifically
; (src/print.asm prn_more_check passes E=$02 to wait_key_timeout, which
; ANDs it against flag 49 and falls through to a plain, untimed wait_key
; when the bit is clear). Nothing in this suite ever armed it before, so
; wait_key_timeout's arm-and-expire path has never run at all - only its
; not-armed fall-through has.
;
; A page fires with a 1-second timeout armed and NOBODY PRESSES A KEY:
; the countdown must expire on its own and set flag 49 bit 7 on top of
; the bit 1 that armed it, so 2 + 128 = 130.
;
; The disarm is a SEPARATE entry on purpose. An EQ that fails aborts its
; entry, so a TIME 0 0 sitting after the assertion would be skipped on
; exactly the run that failed - leaving the pager timeout armed for
; everything after this check, including the SUITES DONE pause below.
; The witness flag carries the verdict across the entry boundary instead.
> _ _   LET 123 104
        LET 127 0
        TIME 1 2
        MES "104 PAGER TIMEOUT - DO NOT PRESS A KEY#n"
        MES "51#n52#n53#n54#n55#n56#n57#n58#n59#n60#n"
        MES "61#n62#n63#n64#n65#n66#n67#n68#n69#n70#n"
        MES "71#n72#n73#n74#n75#n76#n77#n78#n79#n80#n"
        MES "81#n82#n83#n84#n85#n86#n87#n88#n89#n90#n"
        EQ 49 130               ; bit 1 still armed, bit 7 = it expired
        LET 127 1
> _ _   TIME 0 0                ; ALWAYS disarm, whatever happened above
        EQ 127 1
        EQ 123 104
        MES "104 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "104 F#n"
; ---- done-marking checks (105-108) ----
; Appended after 103-104 under the same placement rule: nothing before
; here shifts. These four close the coverage gap that let MOVE and
; PICTURE go years without marking the table done on every path (owner
; ruling 2026-08-04) - no check we or Uto run touched it, which is
; exactly why it survived.
;
; MESSAGE-TABLE CEILING - READ BEFORE ADDING ANY MORE MES LITERALS.
; This fixture sat at EXACTLY 255 user messages (the byte-wide count's
; ceiling) before these checks landed, so every literal added from here
; on SPILLS. DRF handles it silently and correctly: the overflow
; literals are allocated as SYSTEM messages instead and the emitted
; opcode changes from MES 77 ($4D) to SYSMESS 54 ($36). Verified in the
; compiled DDB, not assumed - "106 F" through "SUITES DONE" are now
; SM61-SM66, plus SM67/SM68 for /PRO 14's and /PRO 18's literals, and
; the header's sysmess count went 61 -> 69 so h_sysmess's bounds check
; still passes. No behavioural change: SYSMESS and MES share cprops
; typing ($81, action, marks done) and the same print_msg tail, so
; /PRO 18's "MES stamps done" premise for check 71 still holds. The
; margin is thin though - the interpreter's own highest system message
; is SM60 (src: SM12..SM60), i.e. the spill begins one slot above it.
;
; HOW THEY WORK. ISDONE reads ONE accumulating cell, so an ISDONE placed
; straight after the condact under test would read every earlier stamp
; in this table too (every MES above has set it). A PROCESS push is the
; only thing that CLEARS the cell, and the matching pop leaves it alone,
; so a helper table whose ONLY condact is the one under test reports
; that condact's stamp and nothing else. Same instrument as checks
; 31/32. The helpers are PRO 24-26 at the foot of this file, and the
; caller sets up flag 33 and the location flag HERE, before the push -
; a LET inside the helper is action-typed and would stamp done itself,
; which would make every one of these pass vacuously.
;
; 105 MOVE's SUCCESS arm. Already marked before the ruling (h_move
; called eng_set_done on .match), so this is a regression pin, not new
; behaviour: verb 2 = NORTH, which location 0's connection list has.
> _ _   LET 33 2
        LET 121 0
        PROCESS 24
        ISDONE
        EQ 121 0                ; and it still MOVEd (NORTH 0 -> 0)
        MES "105 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "105 F#n"
; 106 MOVE's NO-MATCH arm - NEW behaviour. Verb 3 = SOUTH, which
; location 0's connection list does not have, so h_move reaches
; .nomatch and fails the entry. Before the ruling that path returned
; c_false without ever stamping, and this printed F.
> _ _   LET 33 3
        LET 122 0
        PROCESS 25
        ISDONE
        MES "106 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "106 F#n"
; 107 MOVE's VERB-BAIL arm - NEW behaviour, and the exit neither
; reference reaches the same way: msx2daad's "if (flags[fVerb]<14)"
; guard falls through to checkEntry = false and the dispatcher stamps
; anyway, while jdaad has no guard at all. Verb 20 is >= 14, so h_move
; bails at "cp 14 / jp nc, c_false" before it reads the flag - which is
; why this reuses PRO 25's MOVE 122 unchanged. The EQ pins that the
; flag came back untouched; it cannot tell the bail apart from .nomatch
; (location 1 has no connections, so that arm would leave 1 as well,
; and both arms mark done now by design) - it is the bail that runs.
> _ _   LET 33 20
        LET 122 1
        PROCESS 25
        ISDONE
        EQ 122 1
        MES "107 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "107 F#n"
; 108 PICTURE marks done - NEW behaviour. Same missing-art premise as
; check 64: sd\SUITE\ holds no 200.*, every probe in the extension
; chain misses, gfx_load fails clean and h_picture reports false, so
; PRO 26's single entry aborts and the table ends. Before the ruling
; PICTURE stamped done NOWHERE (ovl2_true/ovl2_false touch carry only,
; and its cprops row is condition-typed so the dispatcher stamps
; nothing either), and this printed F. The success arm cannot be
; asserted from this leg - the suite deliberately stages no art at all
; - but h_picture stamps at ENTRY, so success and failure are the same
; single instruction and this check covers both.
> _ _   PROCESS 26
        ISDONE
        MES "108 OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "108 F#n"
; ---- article-stripping checks (109-110) ----
; Same placement rule as 81-108: appended after them, so nothing before
; here shifts. NOTHING in this suite tested article handling at all,
; which is exactly how the defect below survived to silicon.
;
; THE RULE (owner ruling 2026-08-05):
;   LIST output strips NOTHING - LISTOBJ/LISTAT/inventory print the
;   object text whole, article and all. Confirmed on real hardware by
;   the owner against DSNEXTE3.BIN, DAAD Ready's own ZX Next
;   interpreter. NextDAAD ran ONE state machine for both callers and
;   discarded every character up to the first space, so "a pair of
;   dungarees" listed as "pair of dungarees" - and a text with NO
;   space in it printed nothing at all, on either path.
;   SUBSTITUTION ("_" in a message) strips a leading "a ", "an ",
;   "some " or "the " and nothing else, case-insensitively. A first
;   word that is NOT one of those survives: jDAAD removes the first
;   word whatever it is (jdaad.js:1588), which destroys descriptive
;   text, and NextDAAD deliberately does not - it follows msx2daad's
;   article-only rule (daad_msg.c:127-133), plus "some " per DAAD
;   Ready's manual and "the " per the owner's 323-text corpus survey.
;
; BOTH ARE VISUAL, like checks 35-38: no condact reads printed text
; back, so the OK line only says the check RAN. Score them by reading
; the lines - each printed name must equal the [expected] beside it,
; character for character. A " F" is impossible here by construction.
;
; The '@' (capitalising) escape cannot be covered from this fixture at
; all: print.asm's prn_decoded treats '@' as an ordinary character
; unless header byte 1 bit 0 says the database is SPANISH, and this
; fixture is compiled EN. It needs a Spanish DDB - owner leg.
> _ _   SETCO 0
        MES "109 SUB [brass lamp] = _#n"
        SETCO 1
        MES "109 SUB [old box] = _#n"
        SETCO 10
        MES "109 SUB [silver keys] = _#n"
        SETCO 11
        MES "109 SUB [rusty anvil] = _#n"
        SETCO 12
        MES "109 SUB [somersault rope] = _#n"
        SETCO 13
        MES "109 SUB [iron gate] = _#n"
        MES "109 OK#n"
; 110 the LIST half. Location 1 rather than 0, because check 100 leaves
; it EMPTY ("PLACE 8 0 - leave location 1 empty behind us") and nothing
; between there and here parks anything on it - so the three names below
; are the WHOLE listing and a fourth line is itself a failure. Location
; 1 doubles as "inside object 1", the box, which is why the fixture has
; always been careful with it; the PLACE 8 0 below is belt and braces
; against a future edit that forgets. Flag 53 bit 6 clear = one object
; per line, which is also the default.
> _ _   PLACE 8 0
        PLACE 10 1
        PLACE 11 1
        PLACE 12 1
        LET 53 0
        MES "110 LIST expect: some silver keys | rusty anvil | somersault rope#n110 LIST actual, one per line and nothing else:#n"
        LISTAT 1
        MES "110 OK#n"
> _ _   MES "SUITES DONE - KEY FOR E03#n"
        ANYKEY
        PROCESS 5
/PRO 2
> _ _   GET 3
/PRO 3
> _ _   DONE
/PRO 4
> _ _   NOTDONE
/PRO 5
> _ _   PROCESS 6
/PRO 6
> _ _   PROCESS 7
/PRO 7
> _ _   PROCESS 8
/PRO 8
> _ _   PROCESS 9
/PRO 9
> _ _   PROCESS 10
/PRO 10
> _ _   PROCESS 11
/PRO 11
> _ _   PROCESS 12
/PRO 12
> _ _   PROCESS 13
/PRO 13
> _ _   PROCESS 14
/PRO 14
> _ _   MES "E03 UNREACHED F#n"
/PRO 15
> _ _   DOALL 0
        PLUS 113 1
/PRO 16
; REDO check (45): restart this table once, guarded by flag 113.
> _ _   PLUS 113 1
        EQ 113 1
        REDO
> _ _   DONE
/PRO 17
; DOALL capacity-cancel check (49): GET each object at loc 0. AUTOG
; refuses SM27 (MAXCARR 0) and cancels the DOALL after object 1.
> _ _   DOALL 0
        PLUS 115 1
        AUTOG
        DONE
/PRO 18
; XUNDONE check (71) helper: MES stamps done=1, EXTERN 0 7 (XUNDONE)
; clears it, entry falls through its own terminator (no DONE/NOTDONE)
; so the table-end pop propagates the cleared stamp, not a forced one.
> _ _   MES "71 sub ran#n"
        EXTERN 0 7
/PRO 19
; Weight-before-count helper (86): AUTOG each object at loc 0 with both
; capacity and strength at 0. The weight refusal (SM43) leaves the DOALL
; alone, so the counter reaches 2; a count-first refusal (SM27) cancels
; the DOALL and leaves it at 1.
> _ _   DOALL 0
        PLUS 125 1
        AUTOG
        DONE
/PRO 20
; Refused-GET containment (85): with MAXCARR 0 the GET refuses SM27 and
; performs DONE, which pops THIS process and returns to check 85's own
; entry - so the LET below must never run and the assertions after the
; PROCESS 20 call still get to run.
> _ _   GET 3
        LET 124 1               ; unreachable
/PRO 21
; AUTOT containment (96): AUTOT finds nothing, prints its composite
; family message and performs DONE, which pops THIS process and returns
; to check 96's own entry instead of aborting PRO 1. Same pattern as
; PRO 20.
> _ _   AUTOT 1
        LET 126 1               ; unreachable
/PRO 22
; Empty-DOALL containment (99, B14 "caso A"): location 1 holds nothing,
; so the DOALL's INITIAL search finds no object and must cancel with
; NEWTEXT + NOTDONE. The NOTDONE pops THIS process with isDone at 0,
; which check 99 reads back with ISNDONE. A bare DOALL is the whole
; entry on purpose - the table must end on the DOALL itself.
> _ _   DOALL 1
/PRO 23
; Iterated-DOALL containment (100, B14 "caso B"): identical to PRO 22
; except that location 1 holds exactly one object, so the DOALL DOES
; iterate before running out. Exhaustion after iteration must leave the
; table DONE (the isDone stamp DOALL's action typing already set), which
; check 100 reads back with ISDONE. The two PROCesses differ only in
; the world state, which is what makes the pair a real discriminator.
> _ _   DOALL 1
/PRO 24
; Done-marking helper (105): MOVE's success arm, alone in its table.
; ONE condact and no other - see the checks' own header for why
; anything else here (a LET, a MES) would make them pass vacuously.
> _ _   MOVE 121
/PRO 25
; Done-marking helper (106 and 107): MOVE's two failing arms. Which one
; runs is decided by flag 33 in the caller - verb 3 reaches .nomatch,
; verb 20 bails at the "cp 14" guard without reading flag 122 at all.
> _ _   MOVE 122
/PRO 26
; Done-marking helper (108): PICTURE with no art staged. The condition
; fails, so this entry aborts and the table ends on it; the isDone the
; pop propagates is whatever h_picture stamped.
> _ _   PICTURE 200
/END
